  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Linked and Advanced Boat Journeys",
    "description": "Combine river-speed ideas across multi-stage trips, linked towns, two boats, and exam-style transfer problems.",
    "teachingApproach": "Treat each advanced problem as a sequence of familiar pieces. Label each leg as downstream or upstream, turn it into a speed or time, and only then connect the conditions. Reuse the chapter’s strongest integrated patterns so the learner practises choosing the right model rather than memorising another formula.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 30,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 19, Boats and Streams. Approved scope: Use integrated source-shaped applications rather than a new formula. Cover comparing a cyclist’s round trip with a boat’s, downstream travel followed by a partial upstream return, three equidistant towns on one river, two boats sharing the same current, speed-ratio questions that feed a later distance calculation, the chapter pattern where doubling the rowing rate changes the upstream/downstream time gap, same-time downstream/upstream distance conditions, and a capstone that combines two mixed journey facts. Learners should identify the effective speed on each leg before calculating anything."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's combine the chapter's ideas.\n\nThe difficult boat questions usually do not need a new formula. They hide two or three familiar moves inside one story. Our job is to separate the legs before joining them again.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A cyclist and a boat travel from A to B and back.\nCyclist speed = 12 km/hr both ways.\nBoat still-water speed = 10 and current = 4, so boat speeds are 14 and 6.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "The cyclist's round-trip average stays 12.\nBoat average = 2×14×6/(14+6) = 8.4 km/hr.\n\nSame route, lower whole-trip average: the boat returns later.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
